<h2 id="how-heading">How Roller Conveyors Work</h2>
<p>Every roller conveyor shares the same basic structure: cylindrical rollers rotate on bearings seated in side frames. Products ride on top of the rollers. The rollers either spin freely (gravity) or are driven by an external force (powered). Frame construction uses formed steel channel or structural members, and the frame gauge rises with load rating.</p>
<h3>Roller Construction</h3>
<p>A conveyor roller is a steel, stainless, aluminum, or plastic tube with a bearing at each end and an axle (often hex) that seats in the frame. Diameter and wall thickness rise with load. Hytrol's chain-driven line shows the spread: the 199-CRR uses 2-1/8-inch diameter rollers with a 1/8-inch wall, the <a href="https://www.cisco-eagle.com/category/3251/26-crr-heavy-duty-straight-chain-driven-conveyor" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">26-CRR</a> uses 2-5/8-inch by 7-gauge rollers, and the 36-CRRH uses 3-1/2-inch rollers with a 0.300-inch wall. Package conveyors commonly use 1.9-inch rollers.</p>
<h3>Roller Spacing (Pitch)</h3>
<p>Roller pitch (center-to-center spacing) determines how many rollers contact the product at any time. <a href="https://blog.hytrol.com/belts-vs-live-roller-conveyors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hytrol's guidance</a> is that a product should be supported by 3 rollers at all times. Hytrol notes that 1.9-inch rollers are most often spaced on 3-inch centers, with 2-inch spacing growing as e-commerce products get smaller. Closer pitch gives smoother transport for small or light items. Wider pitch reduces roller count and suits long, heavy products such as pallets.</p>
<h3>Bearing Types</h3>
<p>Conveyor roller bearings come in several grades. Precision ball bearings (lowest rolling resistance) suit <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">gravity roller conveyors</a> where minimal force must move the product. Standard ball bearings work for <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">powered roller conveyors</a> where the drive overcomes bearing friction. Sealed, lubricated-for-life bearings are common in <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food and beverage</a> applications where re-lubrication adds contamination risk.</p>
<h3>Frame Types</h3>
<p>Roller conveyor frames use three common profiles. Channel frame (C-channel) offers a high strength-to-weight ratio for heavy-duty applications. Angle frame (L-shaped) provides a <a href="/conveyor-types/low-profile-conveyors/">low-profile</a>, economical option for light-duty gravity sections. Structural tube frame suits custom fabrications and <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary stainless steel designs</a>. Specify between-frame width from your widest product plus side clearance, and elevation from the equipment it connects to.</p>
