<p>Accumulation entries reflect Hytrol's published line: belt-driven, line-shaft, and chain-driven models are all offered in zero-pressure versions (see <a href="https://hytrol.com/products/transport/live-roller-conveyor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hytrol live roller conveyors</a>). Speed and load ratings vary by model, so compare supplier data sheets for the specific models quoted. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">cost guide</a> for cost factors.</p>
<h2 id="applications-heading">Roller Conveyor Applications</h2>
<h3>Case and Carton Transport</h3>
<p>Roller conveyors are common for case transport in <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line applications</a>. Sealed cases with flat bottoms ride smoothly across rollers. End-of-line systems typically use <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">powered roller conveyors</a> between case packers, case sealers, labelers, and palletizers. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation zones</a> <a href="/conveyor-types/buffer-conveyors/">buffer cases</a> before palletizing.</p>
<h3>Distribution and Fulfillment</h3>
<p>Distribution centers use <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR roller conveyors</a> for zone-controlled transport and accumulation. Totes and shipping cartons route through pick modules, merge onto trunk lines, and accumulate before shipping sorters, where <a href="/conveyor-functions/singulation/">singulation</a> spaces them for induction. MDR provides zero-pressure accumulation without compressed air, and zones that are not carrying product stop. Large sites build these lines into <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems</a>.</p>
<h3>Pallet Handling</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR conveyors</a> handle loaded pallets between stretch wrappers, pallet jacks, and truck loading areas. Pallet-duty CDLR uses large-diameter, heavy-wall rollers and close support spacing. As an example, Hytrol's <a href="https://acgconveyors.com/partners/hytrol/chain-driven-live-roller-conveyors/36-crrh-heavy-duty-chain-driven-live-roller/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">36-CRRH</a> uses 3-1/2-inch rollers with a 0.300-inch wall and is rated at 2,000 lbs per linear foot with supports on 5-foot centers. <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">Pallet conveyor systems</a> often include <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">right-angle transfers</a> and <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">vertical reciprocating conveyors</a> for multi-level facilities.</p>
<h3>Food and Beverage</h3>
<p><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food and beverage facilities</a> use stainless steel roller conveyors with sealed bearings and open-frame construction for washdown cleaning. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Sanitary roller conveyors</a> feature tool-free roller removal and frame surfaces that shed water.</p>
<h3>Loading Docks and Shipping</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">Gravity roller conveyors</a> are a standard choice for loading dock applications. Workers push cases down a slight decline into trailers. Flexible, expandable gravity roller sections extend into trailers and retract when not in use. They cost less than powered systems and need no electrical infrastructure.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">How to Select a Roller Conveyor</h2>
<p>Selecting the right roller conveyor requires matching six parameters to available equipment.</p>
<p><strong>1. Product bottom surface.</strong> Roller conveyors require a flat, rigid bottom surface. Hytrol notes that bagged materials need the full support of a belt conveyor. The product bottom must span at least 3 rollers at the chosen pitch.</p>
<p><strong>2. Product weight.</strong> Product weight per foot sets roller diameter, wall thickness, bearing type, frame gauge, and support spacing. Light cases ride on light-gauge package rollers. Loaded pallets need heavy-wall pallet rollers in structural frames. Ask for the conveyor's rating in pounds per linear foot and per roller, and check it against your heaviest product.</p>
<p><strong>3. Speed and throughput.</strong> Calculate required speed from your throughput formula. <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">Gravity systems</a> provide variable, uncontrolled speed. Powered systems provide consistent, adjustable speed. Match the conveyor speed to your upstream and downstream equipment rates.</p>
<p><strong>4. Accumulation requirement.</strong> If products must queue without backpressure, select <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR</a> or a zero-pressure <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation conveyor</a>. Continuous transport without accumulation allows simpler <a href="/conveyor-types/live-roller-conveyors/">live roller</a> or gravity configurations.</p>
<p><strong>5. Environment.</strong> Wet or <a href="/conveyor-types/washdown-conveyors/">washdown environments</a> require stainless steel rollers, sealed bearings, and corrosion-resistant frames. Standard warehouse environments use galvanized or painted carbon steel. High-temperature environments require special bearing lubricants and heat-resistant materials.</p>
<p><strong>6. Budget.</strong> <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">Gravity roller</a> costs the least per foot. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR</a> costs more upfront but removes compressed air for accumulation and stops idle zones. <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR</a> costs more due to heavier construction but handles loads that lighter conveyors cannot. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for factor-by-factor cost breakdowns.</p>
<h2 id="evaluate-heading">How to Evaluate Roller Conveyor Options</h2>
<p>Quotes for the same line can differ in roller gauge, support spacing, controls, and who installs and integrates the system. Ask each supplier the same questions so the bids compare:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Load rating:</strong> rated capacity in pounds per linear foot and per roller, and the support spacing that rating assumes.</li>
<li><strong>Roller and pitch:</strong> roller diameter, wall, axle, bearing type, and roller centers, checked against the 3-roller rule for your shortest product.</li>
<li><strong>Drive and controls:</strong> drive type, speed range, zone length, and how the conveyor interfaces with your line controls.</li>
<li><strong>Utilities:</strong> whether accumulation needs compressed air, and at what pressure.</li>
<li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> wear parts (belts, O-rings, chain, motor rollers), lubrication intervals, and spare parts lead time.</li>
<li><strong>Safety:</strong> guarding at pinch points and drives, and conformance to <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Scope:</strong> whether the bid covers supports, installation, controls integration, and start-up.</li>
</ul>
<p>To find suppliers, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-manufacturers/">conveyor manufacturers directory</a>.</p>
