<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What are the different types of roller conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>The five main roller conveyor types are: <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">gravity roller</a> (unpowered), belt-driven live roller or BDLR (one motor drives a belt that turns the rollers by friction), line-shaft (a rotating shaft drives each roller through an O-ring band), <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR</a> (low-voltage DC motors inside some rollers, each driving one zone), and <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR</a> (roller chain and sprockets drive the rollers, used for pallets and heavy loads).</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">How do I choose between gravity and powered roller conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Choose <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">gravity roller</a> when the layout allows a slight decline or a manual push, product weights are consistent, and you do not need speed control or <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation</a>. Set the decline by testing your lightest and heaviest products. Choose <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">powered roller</a> when you need consistent speed, uphill transport, accumulation, or integration with <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated systems</a>. Gravity roller costs less per foot because it has no motor, drive, or controls.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What roller spacing do I need?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Hytrol's guidance is that a product should be supported by 3 rollers at all times. Divide your shortest product length by 3 and choose a roller center at or below that figure. A 12-inch case needs roller centers of 4 inches or less (12 / 3 = 4). Hytrol notes that 1.9-inch rollers are most often spaced on 3-inch centers, with 2-inch spacing growing for small e-commerce products.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What is the weight capacity of a roller conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Capacity is rated per linear foot and per roller, and depends on roller diameter and wall, axle, bearings, frame, and support spacing. Hytrol's chain-driven ratings show the range: 199-CRR at 150 lbs per foot, 26-CRR at 300 lbs per foot on 10-foot supports or 1,000 lbs per foot on 5-foot supports, and 36-CRRH at 2,000 lbs per foot on 5-foot supports. Ask suppliers for both ratings for your heaviest <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet</a> or case.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">Why is MDR replacing line-shaft conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR</a> places a low-voltage brushless DC motor inside one roller per zone. Each zone runs only when product needs to move, so MDR provides zero-pressure accumulation without compressed air, a continuously running line shaft, or a long drive belt. That makes it a common choice for new accumulation and zone-control lines carrying lighter unit loads. Line-shaft and belt-driven conveyors still suit simple continuous transport.</p>

</div>
</div>
